{"archetypes":[{"name":"ci-tooling","focus_area":"risk-integration","weight":1,"rationale":"The diff adds new Python CI jobs, Makefile targets, dependency caches, and ship-pr failed-job recognition that must compose with the existing CI contract.","prompt_body":"Investigate whether the new Python lint and test jobs are wired consistently across GitHub Actions, Makefile targets, requirements files, and scripts/ci-failed-jobs.sh. Check for job-name drift, missing prerequisites, cache path mistakes, working-directory assumptions, and any way a failing Python job could be misclassified by the existing ship-pr flow. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"stdlib-boundary","focus_area":"architecture","weight":1,"rationale":"The plan depends on a strict runtime stdlib-only boundary while colocating dev-only tests and linter dependencies in the same flat python tree.","prompt_body":"Review the new python runtime modules and test_stdlib_only enforcement for import-boundary gaps. Pay attention to nested imports, sibling-module resolution, test/runtime discovery rules, and config choices that could let non-stdlib dependencies leak into runtime code. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"process-wrappers","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The foundation introduces injectable subprocess, git, gh, and agent wrappers whose argv construction and parsing will become shared migration primitives.","prompt_body":"Examine proc.py, git.py, gh.py, agents.py, and their tests for command construction, typed result parsing, timeout behavior, cwd/env handling, and stub-runner fidelity. Look for mismatches between the API promised by the plan and the actual behavior future ship-pr phases would rely on. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"retry-idempotency","focus_area":"risk-integration","weight":1,"rationale":"The gh wrapper introduces per-operation retry behavior where incorrect retries on mutations could duplicate comments, reruns, merges, or PR creation.","prompt_body":"Focus on retry.py and gh.py retry policy boundaries. Verify that transient classification matches the shell precedent, idempotent reads are retried safely, mutating operations are not retried accidentally, and PR creation deduplication cannot mask real failures. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"redaction-parity","focus_area":"security","weight":1,"rationale":"The new redact.py replaces security-sensitive shell redaction logic and must preserve fail-closed behavior and idempotence exactly.","prompt_body":"Review redact.py and test_redact.py against the plan's stated secret families, tmpdir/operator-path rewrites, PEM truncation behavior, and idempotence requirement. Look for regex ordering, multiline handling, punctuation boundary, or replacement-marker bugs that could leak secrets or over-redact useful diagnostics. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"waterfall-launch","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The agents module ports launch-failure classification and first-fixer waterfall behavior from shell code, a likely source of subtle control-flow drift.","prompt_body":"Inspect agents.py and test_agents.py for parity with external_classify_launch_failure and the intended run_ci_fix_vendor tier loop. Check classification token coverage, health-vs-non-health short-circuit behavior, timeout/refusal parsing, and whether launch argv construction preserves each tool's expected invocation shape. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
